ENGLISH 17

&
\newpage
\begin{verbatim}
%
% RULE RObjReciproSubst
< m1: CLAUSE{CLAUSErec1}
             [ mu1,
               predrel/VERBP{VERBPrec1}
                      [ mu3,
                        I3:rel2/I4::NPVAR{NPVARrec1},
                        mu4 
                      ],
               mu5
             ] 
 SUBST:
 m2: NP{NPrec1}[I1:sig1]
>

< m : CLAUSE{CLAUSErec1}
             [ mu1,
               predrel/VERBP{VERBPrec1}
                   I7:[ mu3,
                        I3:rel2/NP{NPrec2}[I2:sig2],
                        mu4 
                      ],
               I5:mu5
             ] 
>

   MATCHCONDITIONS
      <
        I1: EXIST (sig1, [head/RECIPRO{RECIPROrec1}])
        I2: EXIST (sig2, [head/RECIPRO{RECIPROrec1}])
        I3: rel2 IN [objrel, indobjrel]      
        I4: NPVARrec1.index = LEVEL
        I5: NOT EXIST (mu5, [rel1/I51::NP{NPrec3}[mu8]]) AND
            NOT EXIST (mu5, [rel1/PREPP{PREPPrec1}
                                 [mu7, objrel/I51::NP{NPrec3}[mu8]]
                            ] )
          I51: NPrec3.NPhead = reciproNP
        I7: RIGHTTOLEFT (* intentionally absent in generation *)
      >

   PARAMETERS
      <
       LEVEL
       antecedent     : indextype
       multitudepar   : onetype
      >

<
   SUBRULE (* 1. ant = subjrel *)
      < mu1: [mu2, I5:subjrel/T1, mu6] >
      < mu1: [mu2, I8:subjrel/T1, mu6] >

   MATCHCONDITIONS
      <
        I5: (T1.CAT IN [NPVAR, CNVAR] 
                    WITH T1.REC.index = antecedent)  AND
            QUOTE_reciproant(T1)
        I8: T1.CAT IN [NPVAR, CNVAR] AND
            QUOTE_reciproant(T1)
      >

      COMP
         <
          C1: multitudepar = RECIPROrec1.one AND
              NPVARrec1 = COPYT_NPtoVAR(NPrec1) 
                                EXCEPT FOR [cases] AND
              NPrec1.cases = [] AND
              NPrec1.NPhead = reciproNP AND
              NPrec1.generic = nogeneric 
            C11: CLAUSErec1.deixis = omegadeixis AND
                 NPrec1.superdeixis = CLAUSErec1.superdeixis
            A11: QUOTE_AssignCase(NPrec1, sig1, NPrec2, sig2, NPVARrec1.cases)
            C12: CLAUSErec1.deixis <> omegadeixis AND
                 NPrec1.superdeixis = CLAUSErec1.deixis
            A12: QUOTE_AssignCase(NPrec1, sig1, NPrec2, sig2, NPVARrec1.cases)
          A1: NPrec2.superdeixis := omegadeixis;
              NPrec2.generic := omegageneric
         >
      DECOMP
         <
          C1: NPrec2.NPhead = reciproNP AND
              NPrec2.superdeixis = omegadeixis AND
              NPrec2.generic = omegageneric
            C11: CLAUSErec1.deixis = omegadeixis
            A11: QUOTE_AssignCase(NPrec2, sig2, NPrec1, sig1, []);
                 NPrec1.superdeixis := CLAUSErec1.superdeixis
            C12: CLAUSErec1.deixis <> omegadeixis
            A12: QUOTE_AssignCase(NPrec2, sig2, NPrec1, sig1, []);
                 NPrec1.superdeixis := CLAUSErec1.deixis
          A1: NPVARrec1 := COPYT_NPtoVAR(NPrec2);
              antecedent := T1.REC.index ;
              NPVARrec1.index := LEVEL;
              NPVARrec1.generic := nogeneric;
              NPrec1.generic := nogeneric;
              multitudepar := RECIPROrec1.one
         >
>
&

\end{verbatim}
\newpage
\begin{description}
\item[* name:] RObjReciproSubst
\item[* relevant examples:] They gave each other a book
\item[* language:] (Dutch, English, Spanish) English
\item[* kind of rule:] (M-rule, S-rule, Transferrule) M-rule
\item[* subgrammar:] clauseTOsentence
\item[* class:] (name of rule, transformation or filter class) RC\_
ReciprocalSpelling
\item[* date of creation:] 8-SEP-1988
\item[* author:] margreet sanders
\item[* copied from:] Dutch RreciprocalSubst1
\item[* modification:]\mbox{}
\begin{enumerate}
\item date: 16/02/89\ \ \ \ \ author: margreet sanders\\
Adapted for distinction between dualis reading (each other) and multitude 
reading (one another). In transfer, a mapping of the `each other' version to a 
multitude meaning is allowed in analysis only.
\item date: ../../..\ \ \ \ \ author:
\end{enumerate}
\item[* task:] to substitute a reciprocal for an NPVAR which can be related to 
another NPVAR on basis of the attribute `index' of that other NPVAR. 
It is assumed that the reciprocal is a basic expression. 
\item[\ \ strategy:] (also: deviations from accepted proposals) The index of 
the antecedent must be remembered in translation, to check for the correct 
links between the reciprocal and its antecedent. The function ReciproAnt only 
checks for the number of the antecedent NP/CN: it must be plural (NP), or 
singular (for a reciproNP or oneNP) or it must be a set containing plural (for 
CN).\\
The inelegant use of a sig as daughter of the NP is necessary because of the 
function Assigncase, which needs a sig or mu as second and fourth argument.
\item[\ \ rejected alternatives:]
\item[\ \ clarification of variables:]
\item[\ \ clarification of the splitting up into subrules:] There will be more 
subrules, one for every possible position of the antecedent.
\item[\ \ clarification of the collapsing of rules:] (e.g. clarification of parameters)
\item[\ \ differences analytical/generative version:] see below under {\em 
dictionary\/}
\item[\  \ suggested semantics:]
\item[\ \ presuppositions:]\mbox{}
\begin{enumerate}
\item dictionary:\\
In the English pronoun.dict, the genitive forms of the RECIPRO also occur as 
`basic expressions' (each other's, one another's). It is assumed that the NP 
formation subgrammar will prohibit formation of NPs with nothing but such a 
RECIPRO. The attribute {\em one\/} should distinguish {\em each other\/} from {
\em one another\/}. In the dictionary, {\em one another} is accepted in analysis 
only; in the current rule, the same strategy is adopted.
\item rules:\\
It is assumed that in generation, no condition on the order in which successive 
recipros are substituted is needed, since there the parameters going with the 
rule specify only one possible solution.
\end{enumerate}
\item[\ \ consequences in the grammar:]
\item[\ \ translation aspects:]\mbox{}
\begin{enumerate} 
\item within this language:\\

\item in other languages:\\

\end{enumerate}
\item[* relevant literature/documents:]
\item[\ \ remarks:]\mbox{}
\begin{enumerate}
\item problems:\\

\item modifications:\\

\end{enumerate}
\end{description}

\newpage
\begin{verbatim}
% FILTER FpreRecipro1
< m : CLAUSE{CLAUSErec1}
            [ mu1, 
              predrel/VERBP{VERBPrec1}
                     [mu2],
              mu3 
            ]
>
  
   MATCHCONDITIONS
      <
       m: EXIST (mu1,[../I11::NP{NPrec1}[mu4]])        OR
          (EXIST (mu2,[../I11::NP{NPrec1}[mu4]]) AND
           EXIST (mu2,[head/I12::VERB{VERBrec1}[mu5]])
          )                                            OR
          EXIST (mu3,[../I11::NP{NPrec1}[mu4]])
        I11: NPrec1.NPhead = reciproNP
        I12: VERBrec1.reflexivity <> reciprocal
      >

         <
          C1: true
          A1: @
         >

&
\end{verbatim}
